{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>Let us make man in our image, after our likeness.</b> The Ramban wrote in the name of Rabbi David Kimchi that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to the earth, 'Let us make — I and you': for the earth gave the yield of its matter, as it gave for the other living creatures, and the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him the spiritual part; and 'in our image, after our likeness' means that he would resemble them both — in his body, the earth; in his soul, the higher ones." "And that which is written <b>״כִּי בְּצֶלֶם אֱלֹהִים עָשָׂה אֶת הָאָדָם״</b> — to recount the wonder in it that distinguishes man from the other living creatures, and on this explanation many agreed, and yet many further explanations came concerning the saying of 'let us make' and concerning the image and the likeness. And it is proper to say that therefore He used the language of 'let us make,' to show His sovereignty, blessed be He, specifically when He created man, to indicate that the essence of His kingship, blessed be He, is manifest in the creations — in the man whom He fashioned for His glory. And the matter of the image and the likeness: even though it is said (Isaiah 40:18) ״וּמַה דְּמוּת תַּעַרְכוּ לוֹ״, and it is written (Isaiah 40:25) ״וְאֶל מִי תְדַמְּיוּנִי וְאֶשְׁוֶה״ — none the less, we find that the Holy One, blessed be He, appears to His prophets in the likeness of the form of a man, for at Mount Sinai He appeared as an elder sitting in a session, and upon the sea He appeared as a mighty warrior, and it is written (Ezekiel 1:26) ״וְעַל הַכִּסֵּא דְּמוּת כְּמַרְאֵה אָדָם״ — for in all these likenesses He, blessed be He, is accustomed to appear, and concerning this it may be that He said 'in our image, after our likeness,' even though in truth He, blessed be He, has no likeness, and כְּבוֹד אֱלֹהִים הַסְתֵּר דָּבָר." "<b>And they shall rule over the fish of the sea and over the bird of the heavens and over the beast.</b> In the portion of Noah (Genesis 9:2) the order was reversed, as it says: ״וּמוֹרַאֲכֶם וְחִתְּכֶם יִהְיֶה עַל כָּל חַיַּת הָאָרֶץ וְעַל כָּל עוֹף הַשָּׁמַיִם וּבְכָל דְּגֵי הַיָּם״. And so David reversed the order and said (Psalms 8:7–8): ״תַּמְשִׁילֵהוּ בְּמַעֲשֵׂה יָדֶיךָ, כֹּל שַׁתָּה תַחַת רַגְלָיו, צֹנֶה וַאֲלָפִים כֻּלָּם וְגַם בַּהֲמוֹת שָׂדָי, צִפּוֹר שָׁמַיִם וּדְגֵי הַיָּם, עֹבֵר אָרְחוֹת יַמִּים״. Furthermore, here it says ״וְיִרְדּוּ״, while in the portion of Noah he did not mention the language of ruling, and likewise David said ״תַּמְשִׁילֵהוּ״ — and is not this change a matter?" "And what seems close to me to say regarding this is: in these three species there is a varying degree of power in man's hand to rule over one more than another. For with beasts and wild animals and every creeping thing upon the earth, he can rule over them more, for they are found together with him and man can follow after them to all the places where they go and roam and run — which is not so with birds and fish, for man cannot fly in the air nor descend into the depths of the sea in his earthbound condition; none the less he has greater power over birds than over fish, for at least man can see to which place they fly and pursue them or send his arrows and strike them — which is not so with fish, which are hidden in the sea and have a twofold disadvantage. Therefore here, where it says ״וְיִרְדּוּ״ — which in its meaning carries the connotation of descent, for if he is not meritorious he becomes degraded before them and has no power over them at all — it mentioned them in the manner of 'not only this, but even that,' and placed the fish of the sea first: for not only will he not rule over them — but this is not so great a novelty, since he cannot go to the place where the fish go, and his eye does not govern them — but even over the birds, which he can see when they flee from before him, none the less he will not govern them; and not only over birds, but even over the beasts he will not govern. But in the portion of Noah (Genesis 9:2) and likewise David the king (Psalms 8:7–9), they did not mention the language of ruling but only the language of fear and subjugation and dominion, which do not carry in their meaning the connotation of descent — if so, they are surely speaking of a time when man is meritorious, and then he rules over them. Therefore they mention them in the manner of 'not only this, but even that' and reversed the order: not only will he rule over those that walk upon the earth, but even over the birds; and not only over the birds, which he can at least see, but even over the fish — and it is easy to understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:33:50.635702988Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}